---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import Param from "../../../components/Param.astro";
---

<DocsLayout
  docKey="pyramid"
  title="Pyramid"
  description="Deptheos's default stacking method: when to choose the pyramid, what its Smoothness setting does to the picture, and how it blends the frames level by level."
  hasLabs
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>Pyramid</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      The pyramid method, the default, splits every frame into levels of detail, from fine texture to broad
      shading, and blends the frames separately in each level, giving the most weight to the sharpest.
    </p>
    <p>
      Choose it for fine detail at many depths, such as hairs, bristles and antennae in front of a distant
      background, and for subjects with jumps in depth. It reads each frame once, so it is also the quickest of
      the three methods. Its weak spots are plain out-of-focus backgrounds, which can come out grainy, and dark
      areas beside something bright and out of focus, where some of the bright thing's blur can show as a haze.
      For those, try <a href="/docs/depth-map/">Depth</a>; <a href="/docs/methods/">Choosing a
      method</a> shows the three side by side.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section id="smoothness">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Smoothness</h2>
      <p>
        The method's one setting, from <Param path="ranges.pyramid.smoothness.min" /> to
        <Param path="ranges.pyramid.smoothness.max" />, default
        <Param path="defaults.pyramid.smoothness" />; the slider's ends read <em>Crisp, more
        noise</em> and <em>Calm, softer</em>. It sets how much of each spot the sharpest frame takes. At low
        Smoothness the sharpest frame takes nearly all of it; at high Smoothness the spot is shared among more
        frames, whose noise then averages out.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure
      class="lab"
      id="smoothness-figure"
      data-lab="stops"
      data-assets="smoothness"
      data-stops="s1=1|s4=4, the default|s10=10"
      data-stops-label="Smoothness"
      data-start="s4"
      data-places="antenna=Antenna against green|leg=Spider's leg against the dark"
      data-places-label="Place"
      data-panels="{stop}-{place}.jpg=The result"
      data-center="300,180|300,256"
    >
      <div class="lab-body">
        <div class="pan"><img src="/media/labs/smoothness/s4-antenna.jpg" width="768" height="512" alt="The tip of the hornet's antenna against the green, at the default Smoothness" /></div>
      </div>
      <figcaption>
        Results the app made from the hornet and spider sample stacks, at 100%: two places, each stacked at
        three settings. Flick between the settings and watch the plain area beside the subject.
      </figcaption>
      <div class="lab-why" data-for="s1" hidden>
        <p>
          At 1 the sharpest frame takes nearly all the weight at every spot. In a plain area no frame is sharp,
          so the winner is whichever frame's noise scores highest, a different frame from spot to spot, and the
          area carries their grain.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lab-why" data-for="s4">
        <p>The default sits between: plain areas are calmer than at 1, and fine detail is still crisp.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="lab-why" data-for="s10" hidden>
        <p>
          At 10 the weight is shared among many frames and their noise averages out: plain areas are smooth.
          Fine detail is a touch softer, since frames where it is out of focus now get some weight. And beside
          the spider's pale leg the dark background is a little lighter along the leg: more of the leg's glow.
        </p>
      </div>
    </figure>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Start at the default. <strong>Lower it</strong> when fine detail should be as crisp as it can be, and
        look for grain in plain areas. <strong>Raise it</strong> when a plain background comes out grainy, and
        look for softer fine detail and for a lighter haze where something bright sits against something dark.
        When no one setting suits the whole picture, stack at two and <a href="/docs/retouching/">paint</a> from
        one render over the other. (Depth's <em>Smoothing</em> only fills in uncertain spots of the depth map,
        whereas Pyramid's <em>Smoothness</em> blends more frames together across the whole picture.)
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="levels">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>How it works, briefly</h2>
      <p>
        Each frame is split into a <em>Laplacian pyramid</em>: the frame is blurred and halved again and again,
        and each level holds what one step of blurring and halving removed. Hairs, texture and speckles sit in
        the finest level, the shapes of parts a few levels down, and the whole subject as a blob in the
        coarsest. Adding the levels back up gives the frame again, so each level can be blended on its own and
        the levels still add up to a clean picture.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="pyramid-diagram" id="pyramid-diagram" data-lab="diagram"><div class="figure-loading">Drawing the diagram…</div></figure>
    <p class="caption">
      A crop of one frame of a spider, at half size. Under <em>Gaussian pyramid</em>, that frame at the bottom and
      ever smaller, blurrier copies above it. Under <em>Detail levels</em>, each level's detail: the level minus
      the next smaller one scaled back up. Rebuilding runs down the detail levels: scale up, add the next detail
      level, repeat.
    </p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Being out of focus removes fine detail first, so the frames differ most in the fine levels, where the
        choice matters, and are nearly alike in the coarse ones. At every spot of every level each frame is
        scored on how strong its detail is there, and the frames share the spot by weight: the best-scoring
        frame gets the most, and the others a share that falls steeply as their score falls. Smoothness sets
        how steeply.
      </p>
      <p>
        The fine levels choose more sharply than the coarse ones, which blend more frames and so hide seams, and
        the coarsest level is a plain average of every frame. To keep a bright out-of-focus blur from glowing
        into adjacent dark areas, each frame's coarse weight is also scaled by a <em>fine-detail factor</em> so
        frames that are out of focus around a spot lose weight in the coarse levels there too.
      </p>
      <p>
        Burt and Adelson described the
        <a href="https://www.rctn.org/bruno/public/papers/Laplacian-pyramid-Burt%2BAdelson1983.pdf">Laplacian
        pyramid</a> in 1983, and the same year used it to
        <a href="https://cs.brown.edu/courses/cs129/2020_Fall/labs/lab_compositing/BurtAdelson_LaplacianPyramidBlending_1983.pdf">join
        two photos without a visible seam</a>, by blending them level by level. The pyramid method does the same
        with focus.
      </p>
      <details class="numbers">
        <summary>In numbers</summary>
        <ul>
          <li>
            The halving stops when one more would take the short side below
            <Param path="constants.pyramid.level_floor_px" /> pixels, so the number of levels depends
            on the picture's size.
          </li>
          <li>
            A frame's weight at a spot is its score divided by the best score there, raised to a power. At the
            finest level, that exponent is <Param path="constants.pyramid.exponent_base" /> minus Smoothness
            (<Param path="at.pyramid.default.finest_exponent" /> at the default;
            <Param path="at.pyramid.min.finest_exponent" /> at Smoothness
            <Param path="ranges.pyramid.smoothness.min" />;
            <Param path="at.pyramid.max.finest_exponent" /> at Smoothness
            <Param path="ranges.pyramid.smoothness.max" />), stepping down linearly to
            <Param path="constants.pyramid.coarsest_exponent_ratio" />× that value at the coarsest level.
          </li>
          <li>
            From Smoothness <Param path="at.pyramid.score_blur_from" /> up, each level's scores are
            blurred a little before they are compared, so that one noisy pixel cannot decide.
          </li>
          <li>
            The fine-detail factor is the frame's score at the second finest level, pooled over its
            neighborhood and raised to the power <Param path="constants.pyramid.prior_exponent" />.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </details>
      <details id="mixing">
        <summary>Try blending two frames by hand</summary>
        <p>
          Two frames from the 66-frame hornet stack: frame A has its pale front leg in focus, and in frame B the
          leg is far out of focus. Each row beside the picture is one level, the coarsest at the top, and its
          two tiles show what A and B hold there. Click a tile to take that level of the result from that
          frame.
        </p>
        <figure class="lab" id="mixer" data-lab="mixer" data-assets="mixer" data-patch="215,190">
          <div class="lab-body">
            <div class="pan"><img src="/media/labs/mixer/b.jpg" loading="lazy" width="512" height="384" alt="The hornet's pale front leg against its dark body, out of focus" /></div>
          </div>
          <figcaption>
            The result opens as frame B. Switch the rows to A one at a time from the bottom, then try the
            preset buttons. In the tiles, detail is drawn against mid-gray and strengthened so that it shows.
          </figcaption>
          <div class="lab-why">
            <p>
              In the fine levels A holds edges and texture and B is empty gray, so the finest two or three levels
              from A are enough to make the result look sharp, whichever frame the coarse levels come from.
            </p>
            <p>
              The coarsest row holds only broad light and color, and B's is paler: its out-of-focus leg is spread
              over the dark body beside it. Flick between <em>Fine from A, coarse from B</em> and <em>All from
              A</em> and watch the dark parts next to the leg. That light is the glow the method keeps out by
              taking less of the coarse levels from frames that are blurred there.
            </p>
          </div>
        </figure>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
